.TH ASK 1 "2026-08-01" "ask 0.1.0" "General Commands Manual"
.SH NAME
ask \- put a question through a language model
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ask
.RI [ flags ]
.RI [ message ...]
.br
.B ask replay
.RI [ flags ]
.RI [ session ]
.br
.B ask system
.br
.B ask login
.I openai\-codex
.RI [ flags ]
.br
.B ask logout
.I provider
.br
.B ask auth
.RI [ list ]
.br
.B ask version
.br
.B ask help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ask
sends a message to a language model and writes the answer to standard
output. It is a filter: the answer is stdout, progress is stderr, and the
exit status says what happened, so it composes with the rest of the shell.
.PP
There is no tool loop and no agent loop. The model answers, and the answer
is the product.
.PP
Every run appends to an append\-only JSONL session log. The conversation
sent to the model is a pure function of that log \(em each call is preceded
by a
.I request
event recording the exact normalized request, with the conversation itself
stored as a SHA\-256 digest rather than a second copy.
.B ask replay \-check
recomputes the fold and compares, which is what makes "replays exactly" a
claim that can fail rather than a slogan.
.PP
Anything that is not a command is a message. A word one edit away from a
command is refused as a probable typo rather than sent to the model;
.B \-\-
forces a word through as a message.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B ask \fR[\fIflags\fR] [\fImessage\fR...]
Ask. Arguments are joined with spaces. Piped or redirected standard input
becomes the message, or rides with one inside
.I <stdin>
delimiters when both are given. By default this continues the current
conversation; see
.B \-n
and
.BR \-f .
.TP
.B ask replay \fR[\fIflags\fR] [\fIsession\fR]
Re\-render a session. The argument is a path or a bare session id; with
none, the current session. A damaged log renders as far as it reads and
exits 1;
.B \-check
refuses it outright, because what cannot be read cannot be proved.
.TP
.B ask system
Print the default system prompt on stdout. The default is a value, not a
secret, so extending it is ordinary shell rather than a config format:
.IP
.EX
ask \-S "$(ask system; cat house\-style.md)" "..."
.EE
.TP
.B ask login \fIopenai\-codex\fR [\fIflags\fR]
Store subscription credentials in the auth file.
.B \-from\-codex
imports them from the official Codex CLI and is the usual path.
.TP
.B ask logout \fIprovider\fR
Remove stored credentials for a provider.
.TP
.B ask auth \fR[\fIlist\fR]
List stored credential providers and the state of each token. The auth
file path goes to stderr, the entries to stdout.
.TP
.B ask version
Print the version.
.B \-V
and
.B \-\-version
do the same.
.TP
.B ask help
Print the usage summary.
.B \-h
and
.B \-\-help
do the same.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-a " file"
Attach a file. Repeat for more, and they reach the model in the order
given. What a file is comes from reading it, never from its name: a text
file is inlined with its name, and an image, PDF, audio or video file is
attached. See
.B ATTACHMENTS
below.
.TP
.BI \-m " provider/model"
Model to use, for example
.IR anthropic/claude\-sonnet\-5 .
Defaults to
.BR $ASK_MODEL ,
and when continuing a session, to the model that session was started with.
OpenRouter models keep their own slash:
.IR openrouter/anthropic/claude\-sonnet\-4.5 .
.TP
.BI \-S " text"
System prompt, replacing the default. Defaults to
.BR $ASK_SYSTEM .
An explicitly empty
.B \-S ""
sends no system prompt at all.
.TP
.B \-n
Start a new conversation instead of continuing the current one.
.TP
.BI \-f " file"
Read and append to this session log, keeping a thread wherever the caller
wants it. An existing file is continued; a new one is created. This never
uses the conversation directory's current pointer.
.TP
.BI \-d " dir"
Conversation directory. Defaults to
.BR $ASK_DIR ,
else
.IR ~/.ask/sessions .
.TP
.BI \-effort " level"
Reasoning effort:
.IR off ,
.IR low ,
.IR medium ,
or
.IR high .
The default is the provider's own, with thinking on.
.TP
.BI \-max\-tokens " n"
Maximum output tokens for the answer. Default 16384. An answer cut off at
this limit is reported on stderr and still exits 0 \(em it is output, just
incomplete.
.TP
.B \-json
Emit the raw event stream on stdout instead of the answer.
.TP
.B \-q
Suppress progress on stderr, leaving only the answer.
.TP
.B \-check
.RB ( replay )
Verify the replay invariant and exit.
.TP
.BI \-step " n"
.RB ( replay )
Print the exact provider request logged at this sequence number, with the
conversation reconstituted from the fold its digest stands for.
.SH ATTACHMENTS
.B \-a
attaches a file, and repeats:
.PP
.EX
ask \-a chart.png "what is the trend?"
ask \-a q3.pdf \-a q4.pdf "what changed between these quarters?"
.EE
.PP
What a file is comes from its content. A name is a name, not a type: a
.I .png
holding a shell script is a shell script, and a
.I .txt
holding a PNG is an image. A file whose bytes carry a recognised signature
is that type; anything else that is valid UTF\-8 without NUL bytes is text;
anything else is refused, by name, before it is sent.
.PP
Text files are inlined, labelled with their base name \(em
.B \-a main.go
is
.B cat main.go
with a tag \(em so a session log stays something
.BR grep (1)
and
.BR jq (1)
can read. Images, PDFs, audio and video are attached as bytes.
.PP
Binary arriving on standard input is an attachment too, so no flag is
needed for the common case:
.PP
.EX
screencapture \-x \-t png \- \(bv ask "what is on my screen?"
.EE
.PP
Text on standard input keeps its old meaning and rides inside the message
as evidence.
.SS What each provider carries
.TS
tab(:);
l l.
anthropic:images, PDF
openai, openai\-codex:images, PDF
gemini:images, audio, video, PDF
openrouter:images, PDF, WAV and MP3
.TE
.PP
An attachment a provider cannot carry is refused before the session log is
touched, naming the provider and what it does take. This is checked per
provider, not per model: a model that is merely the wrong model for the job
is the provider's own error to report.
.SS Limits
At most 16 attachments, 16 MB each, 32 MB in one message. They are fixed,
because the bytes are read whole, encoded into the session log, and encoded
again into the request \(em a limit that can be raised from the command line
is a limit that gets raised until something falls over further away.
.PP
Standard input is bounded by the same 16 MB, and crossing it is an error
rather than a truncation. A filter that quietly dropped the tail of its
input would answer a question about the first part of a file and present it
as an answer about the file, and nothing downstream could tell the
difference. In practice the model's context window is the real ceiling and
it is far lower; see
.B EXIT STATUS
2.
.PP
The bytes live in the session log. A photograph makes a large JSONL record,
and the session is correspondingly large; in exchange the log remains one
self\-contained file that still replays exactly, and copying it still copies
everything it means. Session files are created mode 0600.
.SH CONVERSATIONS
A session is one JSONL file in the conversation directory, named by a
sortable id. The
.I current
symlink names the session a bare
.B ask
continues, and is repointed on every run.
.PP
Because continuing is the default, a pipeline inherits whatever was asked
before it. When that matters, use
.B \-n
or give the thread its own file with
.BR \-f .
Every run that continues a session announces which one it joined and how
many turns it already holds.
.PP
Only one process may write to a session at a time; a second is refused.
Two writers would interleave two conversations into one file, and every
digest written afterwards would fail its fold. The lock is an exclusive
.BR flock (2)
on the session file, so the kernel releases it when the writer exits
however it exits: a session is never stranded by a crash, and there is no
lock file to find or delete.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ASK_MODEL
Default for
.BR \-m .
.TP
.B ASK_SYSTEM
Default system prompt, overriding the built\-in one and overridden by
.BR \-S .
.TP
.B ASK_DIR
Default for
.BR \-d .
.TP
.B ASK_AUTH_FILE
Path to the stored credential file. Default
.IR ~/.ask/auth.json ,
created with mode 0600.
.TP
.B ANTHROPIC_API_KEY\fR, \fBOPENAI_API_KEY\fR, \fBGEMINI_API_KEY\fR, \fBOPENROUTER_API_KEY
Vendor API keys. Each is required only for the provider that uses it, and
becomes optional behind a gateway or on Vertex, where the credential lives
in the transport.
.TP
.BI < PROVIDER >_BASE_URL
Point one provider at a corporate gateway, for example
.BR ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL .
.TP
.B ASK_AUTH_URL
OAuth token endpoint for a gateway. Its presence enables bearer
authentication on every provider.
.B ASK_AUTH_CLIENT_ID
and
.B ASK_AUTH_CLIENT_SECRET
are sent as client_secret_post form fields;
.B ASK_AUTH_REFRESH_TOKEN
selects the refresh_token grant instead of client_credentials; and
.B ASK_AUTH_SCOPE
is sent when set. Tokens are held in memory for the life of the process
and never written to disk.
.IP
The endpoint must be
.BR https ,
except on loopback. The request body carries a client secret or a refresh
token, so http would put the long\-lived half of the credential on the wire
in the clear; a gateway inside a corporate network is still reached across
a network. The same rule applies to
.B \-token\-url
at
.B ask login
and to a
.I token_url
already stored in the credential file, and it is checked when the endpoint
is configured rather than only when a token expires.
.TP
.B ANTHROPIC_VERTEX_PROJECT_ID
Route
.I anthropic/
models through Google Vertex AI. Its presence enables Vertex, and
.B CLOUD_ML_REGION
is then required \(em
.IR global ,
.IR us ,
.IR eu ,
or a specific region such as
.IR us\-east5 .
.B ANTHROPIC_VERTEX_BASE_URL
replaces the region\-derived endpoint.
.B ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL
is ignored while Vertex is enabled.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disable colour on stderr. Colour is used only when stderr is a terminal.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.ask/sessions/
Session logs, one JSONL file per conversation, plus the
.I current
symlink.
.TP
.I ~/.ask/auth.json
Stored credentials, mode 0600.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The model answered.
.TP
.B 1
An error: bad usage, a missing key, a provider failure, a corrupt log, or
a turn that produced no text.
.TP
.B 2
The context window is full. This is permanent for that conversation \(em
the same request will fail identically forever \(em so it is told apart
from an ordinary error, and a retry loop can stop instead of grinding.
Start a new conversation with
.BR \-n .
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted. The partial turn is logged as partial and excluded from
context, so the session can be continued and the question asked again.
.SH EXAMPLES
Ask a question:
.PP
.EX
ask what is a monad
.EE
.PP
Use a pipe as the data and the argument as the instruction:
.PP
.EX
git diff \(bv ask "write a commit message"
.EE
.PP
Keep stdout clean and watch progress on the terminal:
.PP
.EX
ask "explain this error" < build.log > answer.txt
.EE
.PP
Continue, then branch off:
.PP
.EX
ask "what is a monad"
ask "give me an example in Go"
ask \-n "unrelated question"
.EE
.PP
Keep a thread of its own, and prove it later:
.PP
.EX
ask \-f review.jsonl "review this diff" < patch
ask \-f review.jsonl "now summarize the risks"
ask replay \-check review.jsonl
.EE
.PP
Extend the default system prompt instead of replacing it:
.PP
.EX
ask \-S "$(ask system; cat house\-style.md)" "draft the release note"
.EE
.PP
Ask about what is on the screen, all of it or a region:
.PP
.EX
screencapture \-x \-t png \- \(bv ask "what is on my screen?"
screencapture \-x \-R0,0,900,300 \-t png /tmp/r.png
ask \-a /tmp/r.png "what is this showing?"
.EE
.PP
Turn a picture of a page into something
.BR jq (1)
can test, with no OCR installed:
.PP
.EX
qlmanage \-t \-s 1400 \-o . report.pdf
ask \-a report.pdf.png 'Return ONLY minified JSON: {"runway_months":0}' > r.json
test "$(jq \-r .runway_months r.json)" \-lt 12 && echo escalate
.EE
.PP
Compare two images \(em two quarters of a report, or two screenshots of a
user interface:
.PP
.EX
ask \-a before.png \-a after.png "List only what changed, one per line."
.EE
.PP
Extract a folder of documents into one CSV, in parallel, keyed by index so
completion order cannot scramble the rows:
.PP
.EX
mkdir \-p out; i=0
for f in *.pdf; do
  i=$((i+1))
  ( ask \-n \-q \-a "$f" 'ONE line of CSV, no header: quarter,revenue,runway' \e
      > "out/$(printf '%03d' $i)" ) &
done
wait; cat out/*
.EE
.PP
Read several documents into one conversation, then ask something no single
one of them answers:
.PP
.EX
for q in q1 q2 q3; do ask \-q \-a $q.pdf "Next report."; done
ask \-q "Across all three, name the trend that most threatens this company."
.EE
.PP
Branch a script on the outcome:
.PP
.EX
if ask \-q "does this log show an OOM?" < kern.log; then ...; fi
.EE
.PP
Prove every session on disk still replays:
.PP
.EX
for f in ~/.ask/sessions/*.jsonl; do ask replay \-check "$f" || echo "$f"; done
.EE
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Progress, reasoning, retries and the closing token count all go to stderr,
so
.B 2>/dev/null
leaves the answer alone. Reasoning renders dimmed when stderr is a
terminal. When stdout and stderr are the same file or terminal, the answer
is not printed twice.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR jq (1)
.SH HISTORY
.B ask
is the conversational core of
.BR mu ,
a file\-based agent, carved out as a filter: the provider layer, the
credential handling and the replay invariant, without the tool loop, the
verifier, or the workspace.
.SH CAVEATS
A word one edit from a command is refused rather than sent to the model.
This is deliberate \(em a typo would otherwise be a paid model call
appended to the live conversation \(em but it means
.B ask verison
must be written
.B ask \-\- verison
to reach the model as a message.
.PP
Switching providers mid\-conversation is allowed and degrades gracefully:
each adapter replays its own opaque reasoning state and ignores another's.
The text of the conversation always survives; the thinking behind it may
not.
